the next speaker  was a member of a step dance team in  college  please everyone welcome benji  i'm really excited to uh be here today  to talk about this topic um over the pa  of course of the past two or three days  if you've attended the workshops uh  we've learned some really amazing  techniques from some of the most  talented people in the our community and  that's really awesome um but here what i  want to do is do though is take a step  back from the technical aspects of our  job and focus on the human interpersonal  aspect um and that's because no matter  how awesome our products and insights  are they won't be a thing if our  non-technical colleagues and clients uh  can't understand them  so to many of our non-technical  colleagues what we do is a complete  mystery um and i've found that it  fosters a strange mindset  data science seems to be able to be to  do both everything and nothing at the  same time  so for certain problems people think  they can do everything they think that  we're magicians or super talented  athletes that can overcome any obstacle  they think there's some off the shelf  deep learning and it's it's always deep  learning way to predict any fluctuation  to classify any document and reduce  human work time by 90 percent  and  while it might be nice to be thought of  this way having these that kind of  expectations is a really big problem  that's because these expectations are  impossible to me and if we can't meet  and achieve them our work is deemed a  failure  on the other hand there are many who  think data science can do nothing and  think that their area of expertise their  workflow is so innately human it  requires so much complicated thinking  that no amount of technology can help  repeatable script instead of hand jam  spreadsheet an app some basic filtering  all of these are too simple for this  bespoke indescribable work  so we all know that the truth lies  somewhere in the middle um there's  hardly any human-centric effort that  can't be aided with technology  can it solve all our problems of course  not can it help things along in ways  both large and small certainly  so the problem we find ourselves in is a  problem of communication  how can we tell our clients and  colleagues what we can do to help while  recognizing and respecting their  capabilities and limitations  beyond telling how do we actually  integrate our findings and products into  their solutions  in the government this can be a  particular challenge  a lot of policy makers tend to be old  school much of the work is people driven  there are countless committees and  hierarchies new ideas need to go through  before they can be implemented  at every step there's someone who can  stymie our efforts that's why it's so  important for all data scientists to be  good communicators but especially those  of us who work in government and who are  trying to enact positive change  all right so so why should you listen to  me about this um i've worked in the  federal space specifically the dod and  intelligence community for over a decade  as both a civilian and a contractor i've  had the opportunity to support  operational and being operational and  policy roles and support a variety of  missions including counter-terrorism and  counter proliferation as well as  supporting supply chain risk management  like i am now  i.t integration  training and even diversity equity  inclusion initiatives um and all my  stops along the way i've had a lot of  communication failures and some  successes so let's look at one of each  one failure and one success and uh well  we can what we can learn from them  so let's start with an example from  early in my career when i was supporting  the diversity inclusion section of the  human resources directorate of a federal  law enforcement agency  at this time and i'm sure still  leadership was very concerned with the  feeling that minority employees were  receiving disproportionately low  performance assessments uh this led to  widespread resentment and distrust of  the process  but it was just a feeling they didn't  have any back data to back it up  um so when i heard about this in a  meeting with the chief  i immediately said i i can see if that's  true let me get to work  um i knew that we could test her  hypothesis that minority employees  receiving disproportionately low scores  through a chi-square test  and so when i give this talk to students  um i go into the details about how i got  the data and so on um but i'll cut to  the chase here uh these scores are not  due to random chats like p-value of  .0001  um and when i got this result i was  really stoked i had the data the chief  wanted and it confirmed her thinking um  i wrote up a report and i put a  presentation together instead of me to  talk to to talk to her  and it was a total dud i tried to  explain it but it just didn't land um  and she went back just to asking me for  the other regular reports i was giving  her  um so  you know why why did it fail um  it took me a long time um and some good  reflection to to come to this but what  i've what i've  settled on was that i just told her i  was going to solve it i didn't actually  talk to her about the results and what  they would actually look like so when i  showed up to her with a p-value there  was nothing for she didn't know what to  do with it  um  i never asked her how she was actually  going to use this information like where  what she was going to do with the  results of of this analysis and i made  it about me it was just a stats report  not part of an integrated solution my  customer could actually use  um so the bottom line for this is that  if your final deliverables of p-value it  probably isn't going to land  um okay so let's turn to another example  at a different agency where i did the  opposite where i was successful  in delivering a data science insight  using uh better communication  and interestingly this problem was  was actually more complicated and had a  more complex solution  and this time the key of success was  working with the clients to understand  their process how it would be used and  how data science could help  so every year this agency would devote a  large percentage of its budget to grants  for r d  but previous year's grants proposals  were kept in a folder as word documents  with just alphanumeric codes as the  names of the as the names of the files  so  basically it was impossible beyond  remembering from past years if a new if  a new proposal was like any formal  proposals  they had no way of comparing past  proposals to new ones no way of knowing  if they had awarded something similar in  the past and if it was a success or  failure or if they had an award or if  they hadn't awarded something similar in  the past how was the new proposal  different how might it actually change  things  so the solution we came up with was to  read all the reports in and use drvec to  take a current grant and compare it to  all previous years grants and get the  top and most similar grants so to to be  fair this we didn't use r for this but  we made a shiny app they use the output  so that's how that's how i'm justifying  using this example here um  so the  it and what was  what was really successful about this is  that the clients actually use the  results of this in the grant awarding  process  um  so why was this successful um  i explained from the very beginning what  the outcome was going to be and how it  could be used  i explained using the minimum necessary  info so like i wasn't talking to them  about like skip gram or bag of words or  things like that but i didn't dumb it  down either um i explained to them that  how what we were doing wasn't magic it  was math um cleverly applied to and that  the technique had been in use now for  for a while  um  and they actually started using the  results of this system in the grant  awarding process now to be fair the  client didn't actually use the app  themselves we did um and i'm sure a lot  of you who have worked in government  space are used to setting up um with  like bi tools like tableau or other  things like that where your end customer  still ends up having you print out the  reports for them to read  um  all right so  what are like the basic  the do's and don'ts then of  communicating data science findings to  to non-experts  um  number one  is understanding your end user so what  are their strengths and weaknesses how  will they use what what you're giving  them  i think if you compare the two case  studies that i've talked about so far  today um  you see that in in one there wasn't  really much of an effort to understand  the process or the end user and the  other there was a lot of care and effort  and that goes a long way in actually  being able to affect change and  get what you're producing into action  um  really important especially in  government is understanding your end  user's boss  this this is so essential because almost  every decision  will require approval or at least  consensus from multiple people and  multiple levels of authority so it's not  just enough to understand your your end  user you need to understand the their  ecosystem if you want your product to be  successful or your insight to be heard  so  number three is don't make it about you  so don't do what you want to do do what  your customer needs does that means  sometimes using a less sexy approach so  you can explain it for sure um  even in the first first circumstance i  talked about today something pretty  basic in our minds wasn't able wasn't  able to land and that's really because i  made that process about me and not about  how my customer could actually use  something  so four be humble  you can only do so much no matter how  smart and talented you are you're part  of a large organization and you have to  understand understand that so we're not  going to change the world in a in a day  and we need to understand how we can  contribute  while staying staying  grounded  so five determine in advance how your  product will be used so this is this is  more tactical but totally vital um  before you get to work work with your  end user to see and deliver a product  that will be usable  ideally in the existing process so in  this case  whereas in the first  example  i did some analysis with no thought of  how the end user would actually apply it  in the second case  i spent a lot of time working with the  end users of  understanding the process and how the  analysis could actually be used  and i think that's why it was actually  successful  six uh bring them along your journey um  and i think what what  what i mean by saying this is keep  people informed as you move along um  this lets your initiative your  insight your product become something  they feel ownership in  and that will help lead to its adoption  and lead to their greater understanding  and last seven is be prepared to defend  your work so take your work seriously  and your customers will too  uh even though this should be a  user-centric process that doesn't mean  they're calling all the shots so  you're the expert you're the expert here  you need to work to understand their  process but  keep in mind who you who you are and  your talents and skill sets  so that's my uh that's my talk thank you  very much and uh please feel free to get  in touch with me to talk about  this type of work in the government  space

